<Site title="Security & privacy" description="Process, network, executable trust, local privacy, release verification, and documented residual risks.">
  <div class="hero prose"><p class="eyebrow">Controls and their limits</p><h1>Security & privacy</h1><p class="lede">Understand what openDownloader checks, what it stores, and where its guarantees stop.</p></div>
  <section class="prose"><h2>Process execution and tools</h2><p>Media tools run with argument vectors rather than shell-string interpolation. Release builds discover executables through explicitly configured paths, the application-local managed directory, or system PATH; they do not search the working directory or repository tree.</p><p>Managed tool downloads are checked against pinned SHA-256 values before installation. Custom executables are code selected by the user; their version command is executed during validation. These checks do not make every executable safe.</p><a href={path('docs/media-tools/')}>Media tool management →</a></section>
  <section class="prose"><h2>Input and network boundaries</h2><p>URL validation permits HTTP(S), rejects malformed inputs and prohibited local/private addresses, and checks DNS results before external-tool delegation. The in-process TikTok fallback validates redirects and pins validated addresses for connections.</p><div class="status"><p><strong>Residual limitation:</strong> preflight validation cannot constrain yt-dlp’s later DNS resolution, redirects, or connections. Private-network protection is not end-to-end for delegated traffic; an enforcing network sandbox or proxy would be required.</p></div></section>
  <section class="prose"><h2>Local data and privacy</h2><p>The application has no account service or analytics integration. It contacts media hosts and configured tool sources. Those services and tools have their own privacy policies and network behavior.</p><p>History is stored locally in SQLite and is not encrypted. Persisted URLs are sanitized; only allowed public identifiers are retained. Some retries require pasting the source URL again. Diagnostics redact sensitive URL and credential details and retain a bounded in-memory buffer.</p><a href={path('docs/privacy/')}>Canonical privacy policy →</a></section>
  <section class="prose"><h2>Known dependency risk</h2><p>The v1.1.0 host’s documented, version-scoped exceptions cover RUSTSEC-2024-0370 (unmaintained build-time proc-macro-error) and RUSTSEC-2024-0429 (GLib runtime unsoundness on Linux GTK). These are accepted residual risks, not fixes. Core has no exceptions; later versions return to strict audit by default.</p><a href={canonical('docs/release-remediation.md')}>Read the full security disposition and re-evaluation requirements →</a></section>
  <section class="prose"><h2>Release integrity</h2><p>The production workflow inspects and smoke-tests the Debian artifact, signs the package and checksum manifest, and creates GitHub build provenance. These provide distinct evidence of integrity, authenticity, and origin.</p><Verification /></section>
  <section class="prose"><h2>Reporting and support</h2><p>No ongoing maintenance or security-response commitment is offered. Consult the repository’s reporting policy and lifecycle statement before relying on support.</p><div class="actions"><a href={canonical('SECURITY.md')}>Security reporting policy →</a><a href={canonical('docs/security.md')}>Full technical security evidence →</a></div></section>
</Site>
